.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt DMED 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dmed
.Nd terminal code editor with AI agents
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl v
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl h | Fl v
.Op Ar directory
.Nm
.Ic setup-ai
.Nm
.Ic --dump-keys Ar format
.Nm
.Ic --dump-tokens
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a terminal-native code editor with AI agents as first-class participants.
AI agents read, propose and apply changes to your code, but humans approve
every diff via diff-preview before anything is modified.
.Pp
When invoked with
.Ar file
arguments, those files are opened in tabs.
When invoked with a
.Ar directory
argument, the project tree sidebar is shown and the fuzzy finder searches
within that directory.
.Pp
If no arguments are given,
.Nm
opens an empty buffer.
.Pp
With the
.Ic setup-ai
subcommand,
.Nm
runs an interactive wizard that walks through the built-in AI provider
presets, tests the connection, and writes the result to
.Pa ~/.dmed.conf .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , -help
Show help message and exit.
.It Fl v , -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl -dump-keys Ar format
Print the key cheat sheet the editor shows, generated from the binding tables
instead of copied out of them:
.Ar md
for Markdown,
.Ar roff
for this page,
.Ar actions
for the action names the
.Sy [keys]
section rebinds.
The action dump is what a document that cannot run
.Nm
still quotes correctly.
.It Fl -dump-tokens
Print the syntax token names a
.Sy [theme]
rule may name, and below them the names that style a wrapper no terminal paints
and are therefore refused.
The same list the editor quotes when it refuses a line.
.El
.Sh KEYBINDINGS
These chords are the defaults.
Each one is an action with a name, so
.Sy [keys]
in
.Pa .dmed.conf
moves it; the protected keys listed there do not.
.Ss Navigation
.Bl -tag -width "Alt+1..9"
.It Sy Arrows
Move cursor
.It Sy Home / End
Line start / line end
.It Sy PgUp / PgDn
Page up / page down
.It Sy Ctrl+Up/Down
Scroll without moving cursor
.It Sy Ctrl+L
Go to line (N, N:C, or +N/-N relative)
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+Left / Ctrl+Alt+Right
Jump back / forward through the jump list: the positions this pane's discrete
jumps came from. A position is recorded by go to definition
.Pq F12 or Ctrl+Click ,
a Find in Project hit,
.Sy Ctrl+L ,
the debugger's stop line and
.Sy Ctrl+Shift+Backspace
below — not by motion the cursor keys already retrace
(arrows, clicks, the in-file find moves, bookmarks, hunks, the bracket pair), so
the list stays short enough to walk. Entries name the file by path, so a back
step reopens a file whose tab was closed meanwhile; the two stacks live in the
pane and are not saved across runs. Where the terminal cannot tell the chord
from
.Sy Alt+Left/Right
(the tab switch), use the
.Dq Edit: Jump Back
and
.Dq Edit: Jump Forward
palette commands.
.It Sy Ctrl+Shift+Backspace
Go to where this file was last changed. The position is the buffer's: every
method that changes text pushes an undo step and the snapshot takes the caret
with it, so typing, the line commands, the transformations and the multi-cursor
edits are all answered without separate bookkeeping. It is where the change
started, which is also what one
.Sy Ctrl+Z
restores, and undoing the change does not clear the answer. Per file, never
across files — the jump list above is what carries the caret between them. The
command records where it left the caret, so
.Sy Ctrl+Alt+Left
returns; a file that was never edited and a caret already standing on the
change are both refusals that move nothing. Where the terminal collapses the
chord into
.Sy Ctrl+H
(Find and Replace), use the
.Dq Edit: Go to Last Change
palette command.
.El
.Ss Editing
.Bl -tag -width "Alt+Up/Down"
.It Sy Shift+Arrows
Select text
.It Sy Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X / Ctrl+V
Copy / cut / paste
.It Sy Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+R
Undo / redo
.It Sy Ctrl+Y / Ctrl+D
Delete line / duplicate line
.It Sy Ctrl+Slash
Toggle comment
.It Sy Alt+Z
Toggle word wrap
.It Sy Alt+B
Toggle inline git blame (who changed each line and when)
.It Sy Alt+Up / Alt+Down
Move line up / down
.It Sy Alt+C / Alt+O
Fold / unfold the block the cursor belongs to. Blocks come from the indentation;
the command answers anywhere inside the block, on the closing brace that stays
visible, and on a signature line whose brace is written on its own line below.
The cursor lands on the block's first line, which carries the gutter chevron;
the hidden lines collapse into a
.Dq … N lines hidden
row that unfolds on a click.
.It Sy Ctrl+Shift+O / Ctrl+Shift+T / Ctrl+Shift+B
Merge conflict under the cursor: keep our side / their side / both. One
resolution is one undo step; markers that do not form a complete block are
left untouched. Also reachable as the
.Dq Merge: Take …
palette commands, which is the route on terminals that cannot tell these
chords from their control bytes.
.It Sy Ctrl+]
Jump to the matching bracket or quote. With the cursor between a pair it goes
to that pair's closing delimiter, so the same chord walks back and forth —
there is no backward chord because
.Sy Ctrl+[
is Escape. A legacy terminal sends one control byte for
.Sy Ctrl+]
and the shifted key, and both names are bound.
.It Sy Ctrl+Shift+]
Select what is between the pair the cursor belongs to, the delimiters excluded;
empty brackets just move the cursor between them. Only brackets are found from
inside a pair — a quote works when the cursor sits on it. Where the terminal
cannot tell the chord from its control byte, use the
.Dq Edit: Select Inside Brackets
palette command.
.It Sy Ctrl+U / Ctrl+Shift+U
Uppercase / lowercase the selection, or the whole buffer when nothing is
selected
.It Sy Alt+Shift+S
Sort the selected lines (the cursor line when nothing is selected): compared
case-insensitively, blank lines last
.It Sy Alt+Shift+T
Trim trailing spaces and tabs from the selected lines, or from the whole file
when nothing is selected
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+J
Pad the selected lines with spaces so their text starts at the deepest column
the block already has; an existing tab indent is never rewritten
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+N
Number the selected lines 1., 2., … after their indentation; blank lines are
skipped and an existing
.Dq N.
marker is replaced, so the command can be repeated
.It Sy Enter / Backspace / Delete / Tab
Standard editing
.El
.Ss Files and Tabs
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+O"
.It Sy Ctrl+S
Save active tab (untitled: Save As)
.It Sy Ctrl+T
Open file by path
.It Sy Ctrl+O / F3
Fuzzy file finder
.It Sy Ctrl+W / Ctrl+X
Close tab (last tab quits)
.It Sy Alt+Left / Alt+Right
Switch tabs
.It Sy Alt+1..9
Jump to tab N
.It Sy Ctrl+Tab
Cycle this pane's tabs in most-recently-used order: one step further per press,
wrapping back through the tab the walk started on, so holding the chord walks
the list. A real focus change (the arrow chords, a click on the tab bar, the
finder) records the recency order; the walk itself does not, or it would
reorder the list under itself and ping-pong between two tabs. It never reopens a
tab you closed. Per pane, not saved across runs. Where the terminal cannot
deliver the chord — a legacy terminal sends the plain
.Sy Tab
(the indent) and Windows Terminal keeps
.Sy Ctrl+Tab
for its own tab bar — use the
.Sy Alt+backquote
alias or the
.Dq View: Cycle Recent Tabs
palette command.
.El
.Ss Splits and Panels
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+Alt+P"
.It Sy Ctrl+\e / F6
Split vertical
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+H / F7
Split horizontal
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+P / F8
Focus other pane
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+W
Close pane
.It Sy Ctrl+B / F9
Toggle project tree
.It Sy Ctrl+G
Git panel (status mode: space stage, a stage all, c commit, d diff,
b branch, f fetch, p push, q close)
.It Sy Alt+T
Toggle terminal
.El
.Ss Mouse
The bottom-left status-bar icons (tree, git, AI, debug, terminal) are
clickable; hovering one shows a callout with its keyboard shortcut.
.Ss Debug
.Bl -tag -width "Shift+F11"
.It Sy Ctrl+Alt+D
Toggle debug panel
.It Sy F4
Toggle breakpoint at cursor line
.It Sy F5
Run / continue
.It Sy Shift+F5
Stop session
.It Sy F10
Step over
.It Sy F11
Step in
.It Sy Shift+F11
Step out
.El
.Ss AI
.Bl -tag -width "Alt+I"
.It Sy Alt+A
Toggle AI chat panel
.It Sy Alt+I
Inline rewrite (select text first)
.It Sy Alt+G
Inline ghost suggestion (Tab accept, Esc dismiss)
.It Sy Alt+L
Agent panel: background task queue, progress, cancel
.It Sy Ctrl+U
Clear chat history
.El
.Ss Search
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+H"
.It Sy Ctrl+F
Find in file
.It Sy Ctrl+H
Find and replace
.It Sy F3 / Shift+F3
Next / previous match
.El
.Ss Git
.Bl -tag -width "Alt+]"
.It Sy D (in panel)
Side-by-side diff vs HEAD
.It Sy Alt+[ / Alt+]
Previous / next merge conflict while the file holds markers, previous / next
hunk once it has none
.El
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+P"
.It Sy F12 / Ctrl+Click
Go to definition (LSP)
.It Sy Ctrl+P / F2 / double Shift
Command palette
.It Sy F1 / Ctrl+E
Help overlay
.It Sy Ctrl+Q / Ctrl+C
Quit
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads configuration from
.Pa ~/.dmed.conf
(global) and
.Pa .dmed.conf
in the project root (overrides global).
Settings hot-reload on save.
.Pp
The configuration file uses INI format with the following sections:
.Ss [editor]
.Bl -tag -width "syntax_theme"
.It Sy tab_width Ns = Ns Ar N
Tab display width (default: 4), for a file no
.Pa .editorconfig
says anything about.
.It Sy syntax_theme Ns = Ns Ar name
Chroma syntax theme name (default: monokai).
A name the registry does not know is a typo, and a typo paints monokai rather than
nothing; the
.Sy [theme]
section recolours single tokens over whichever style is named.
.It Sy line_numbers Ns = Ns Ar bool
Show line numbers (default: true).
.It Sy word_wrap Ns = Ns Ar bool
Wrap long lines to the pane width (default: false; Alt+Z toggles).
.It Sy skipped_dirs Ns = Ns Ar list
Comma-separated directories to skip in tree/finder
(default: .git,node_modules).
.El
.Ss [ai]
.Bl -tag -width "ollama_url"
.It Sy provider Ns = Ns Ar type
AI backend (default: ollama). Wizard presets: Ollama (local), OpenAI,
DeepSeek, Groq, LM Studio (local), vLLM (local), Unsloth (local), Custom; the legacy values
ollama / openai still work.
.It Sy model Ns = Ns Ar name
Model tag (e.g. gpt-4, qwen2.5-coder:7b).
.It Sy ollama_url Ns = Ns Ar url
Ollama server URL (default: http://localhost:11434).
.It Sy api_key Ns = Ns Ar key
API key for OpenAI-compatible providers.
.It Sy system_prompt Ns = Ns Ar text
System prompt for AI conversations.
.It Sy context_max Ns = Ns Ar N
Max runes sent as file context (default: 6000).
.It Sy temperature Ns = Ns Ar N
Generation temperature in tenths (7 means 0.7); 0 uses the provider default.
.It Sy num_ctx Ns = Ns Ar N
Context window in tokens for Ollama (num_ctx); 0 uses the default.
.It Sy num_predict Ns = Ns Ar N
Maximum output tokens; 0 uses the provider default.
.It Sy tool_rounds Ns = Ns Ar N
Cap on the chat tool-calling loop depth; 0 uses the built-in cap (6).
.It Sy allow_run Ns = Ns Ar value
Whether the model may run shell commands (RUN tool): always, never or ask
(default: always).
.It Sy restrict_to_root Ns = Ns Ar bool
Bound READ/EDIT/REPLACE paths to the project root (default: false).
.El
.Ss [agent]
.Bl -tag -width "system_prompt"
.It Sy system_prompt Ns = Ns Ar text
Instruction override for background agents producing edits; empty uses the
built-in prompt.
.It Sy context_max Ns = Ns Ar N
Total size budget in bytes of file context gathered for an agent task
(default: 262144).
.El
.Ss [debug]
.Bl -tag -width "adapter_mode"
.It Sy mode Ns = Ns Ar type
Launch mode passed to the adapter (Delve: debug, test or exec; default: debug).
.It Sy program Ns = Ns Ar path
Package directory, executable or module entry; empty derives the active
file's directory.
.It Sy args Ns = Ns Ar string
Whitespace-separated arguments for the debuggee.
.It Sy stop_on_entry Ns = Ns Ar bool
Pause at program start (default: false).
.It Sy adapter_cmd Ns = Ns Ar path
DAP adapter binary (default: dlv); the legacy dlv_path key is an alias.
.It Sy adapter_mode Ns = Ns Ar mode
reverse (the adapter dials us back) or stdio (stdin/stdout DAP);
default: reverse.
.It Sy adapter_args Ns = Ns Ar string
Extra CLI arguments for the adapter process.
.It Sy launch_type Ns = Ns Ar name
DAP launch "type" field (default: go).
.It Sy launch_request Ns = Ns Ar kind
launch or attach (default: launch).
.It Sy launch_json Ns = Ns Ar json
Raw JSON merged into the launch body; adapter-specific keys override the
built-in ones.
.El
.Ss [ui]
.Bl -tag -width "chat_width_pct"
.It Sy tree_width Ns = Ns Ar N
Project tree sidebar width in columns (default: 25).
.It Sy chat_width_pct Ns = Ns Ar N
Chat panel width as percentage of screen (default: 40).
.It Sy lang Ns = Ns Ar code
UI language: en or ru (default: en).
.El
.Ss [keys]
Each key is an action name and each value the chord, or chords, it should
answer to:
.Dl file.save = ctrl+s, ctrl+1
.Pp
An empty value unbinds the action and a list replaces the chords it answered to,
so two lines naming each other's chord are a swap.
Copying the list back into the file changes nothing: a chord the editor ships with
two meanings \(em F6 steps in the debug panel and splits outside it \(em keeps both
when either half restates it.
The action names and the chords they ship with are listed by
.Ic "dmed --dump-keys" Ar actions .
A line naming an unknown action, a malformed chord or a chord another action
already owns is refused and reported on the status bar instead of being applied
silently.
Esc, Enter, Tab, Backspace, Delete, the arrow and page keys, Home, End and
.Sy ctrl+q
are protected and cannot be moved: overlays, prompts and the review panel are
built around them, and
.Sy ctrl+q
is the exit that is guaranteed when the mouse is unavailable.
Rebinding also re-keys macro playback, because a replayed macro runs the same
dispatch chain as a live key.
.Ss [theme]
Each key is a syntax token name and each value the style that token should be
painted with, laid over the style named by
.Sy [editor] syntax_theme :
.Dl keyword = #ff79c6
.Dl comment = #6272a4 italic bg=#101014
.Dl generic.strong = nobold
.Pp
The grammar is chroma's own: a
.Sy #rrggbb
(a short hex is padded, so
.Sy #f00
is red),
.Sy bg:#rrggbb
, and
.Ar bold ,
.Ar italic ,
.Ar underline
with
.Ar no Ns -
prefixes to take them off.
An empty value switches the rule off and keeps the line.
A rule is merged after the style has resolved a token through its parents, which
is what lets
.Sy nobold
above un-bold a token the style bolded; a token the file says nothing about keeps
the style's own answer.
Names are read in any spelling they are met in
.Pf ( KeywordConstant ,
 keyword.constant ,
 keyword_constant )
and listed by
.Ic "dmed --dump-tokens" .
A line that names no token, or styles a wrapper no terminal paints, is refused and
counted on the status bar rather than kept as a colour that does nothing.
.Pp
The palette command
.Sy Theme: Syntax Colours...
is the same section with a preview: the arrow keys walk the bundled styles, the
token and the entry are typed, and the sample under the rows is painted from what
the rows say, so the file is untouched until
.Sy ctrl+s .
Saving the file from outside is hot-reloaded; the buffers are not re-tokenized for
a colour change, because the cache holds token kinds and the style is asked per
painted run.
.Ss .editorconfig
A
.Pa .editorconfig
beside a file answers for that file and wins over
.Sy [editor] :
.Ar indent_style ,
.Ar indent_size ,
.Ar tab_width ,
.Ar trim_trailing_whitespace
and
.Ar insert_final_newline .
The nearest file wins, and inside it the last section that matches;
.Sy root = true
ends the walk.
.Ar tab_width
and
.Ar indent_size
default each other, so a space file with only a tab stop indents to that stop and
a tab file with only an indent size displays its tabs that wide.
The tab stop is what wrapping, folding and a click to a column are measured in.
The two save rules are applied when the file is written \(em the trim is one undo
step, so
.Sy ctrl+Z
puts the spaces back \(em and to an agent's proposal before it is reviewed, so the
diff that is approved is the file that lands.
Patterns use the editorconfig glob
.Pf ( "* ? [seq] ** {a,b} {1..9}" ) ,
matched case-insensitively on Windows and macOS, and a pattern with no slash
matches the basename.
.Ar end_of_line ,
.Ar charset
and
.Ar max_line_length
are not honoured: the editor writes the line endings and the encoding a file
already uses, and wrapping is a property of the pane.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.dmed.conf"
.It Pa ~/.dmed.conf
Global configuration file.
.It Pa ./.dmed.conf
Project-level configuration (overrides global).
.It Pa .editorconfig
Per-file indentation and save rules, in any directory a file lives in or above
it (overrides
.Sy [editor] ) .
.It Pa ./.dmed-session.json
Session state (open tabs, cursor positions).
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "DMED_DEBUG_KEYS"
.It Ev DMED_PROVIDER
Override the AI provider.
.It Ev DMED_MODEL
Override the default AI model.
.It Ev DMED_OLLAMA_URL
Override the Ollama server URL.
.It Ev DMED_API_KEY
Override the API key for OpenAI-compatible providers.
.It Ev DMED_LANG
UI language: en or ru.
.It Ev DMED_SHELL
Shell used by the terminal panel.
.It Ev DMED_PLUGIN_REPO
Override the plugin store repository (owner/repo).
.It Ev DMED_TEMPERATURE
Override the generation temperature (in tenths).
.It Ev DMED_NUM_CTX
Override the Ollama context window (num_ctx).
.It Ev DMED_NUM_PREDICT
Override the maximum output tokens.
.It Ev DMED_TOOL_ROUNDS
Override the chat tool-calling loop cap.
.It Ev DMED_ALLOW_RUN
Override RUN-tool policy: always, never or ask.
.It Ev DMED_DEBUG_KEYS
When set, recognized keys are echoed into the status bar (debugging aid).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Open a file:
.Pp
.Dl $ dmed main.go
.Pp
Open a project directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ dmed .
.Pp
Open multiple files:
.Pp
.Dl $ dmed src/*.go
.Pp
Configure AI interactively:
.Pp
.Dl $ dmed setup-ai
.Pp
With AI configured for OpenAI:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[ai]
provider = openai
model = gpt-4
api_key = sk-...
.Ed
